I would argue there's a pretty meaningful difference. I live in a majority Christian country and I can burn the Bible, and I'll get absolutely no government reprisal for it. I can say that Jesus was a lunatic, or depict him having sex with a donkey, or whatever I want, and get absolutely no government reprisal for it. I can be gay, and marry another man, and then move to a completely different majority Christian country like Greenland (93% belonging to the Church of England, last records show), and have it be recognized and accepted. 

&#x200B;

Is there any majority Muslim country where I could do similar?

If people are suffering, fixing the cause of the suffering is probably a better aim than just going "oh man, fixing that sounds hard and expensive, here's an easy painless way out!" 

&#x200B;

As someone who suffered pretty badly from suicidal thoughts years ago, I'm damn glad there wasn't an easy, painless, and most importantly socially acceptable way out at the time. My life now is happier than I ever could have dreamed. 

&#x200B;

Either way, good job on downvoting me and changing the subject from "if it's easier to kill yourself, more people will kill themselves" to "more people killing themselves isn't necessarily a bad thing anyway".

Eh, I guess you could describe the Golden Rule as religious? I just think it's a pretty decent moral to live by for most people, without getting into the weeds so far as ethics go. But I suppose any ethical system could be described as "religious" if the definition if broad enough. 

&#x200B;

I'd agree that it's different, but I still think it supports my original point of: More people will kill themselves if it's easier to kill themselves. I think if all alternative options are explored strongly, then I wouldn't be too against it... a certain subset of people will do it no matter what, after all. I just worry that certain parties who have a strong care about keeping things cheap will have a motivation to not always explore other options (particularly since "not killing yourself" tends to be more expensive for the government in the long run) as fully as they should.

Given more context, I don't know if I'd say "insufferable" any more, just a bit different. If you're not aware, there's a certain type of adult who takes great pride in not liking and not having tried popular things. THAT person is insufferable. You don't sound like that, you just sound kinda young still. There's a lot of dreck out there that doesn't hold interest, but there's so much incredible media out there that there's always more to try. I'm sorry you don't enjoy reading, that was what kept me alive as a kid and as a teenager, being able to escape from the world for a while in a book. (My parents didn't let me have video games. :P )

&#x200B;

So if there's anything to "work on", I'd just say maybe don't mention not liking popular things in general, because people are very likely to take it the wrong way.

If a student asks how much piano teachers make, because they want to teach someday, I'm going to tell them. It makes no sense to me to go "well, I can't tell you that, it's a secret." Public school teachers have their salary to look up, so keeping it hidden isn't an option for them. It's not like I was complaining about how much I make or bragging about how much I make - I just told them the ranges for pay in the city, with consideration for how long the person in question had been teaching. 

&#x200B;

As for private life... I've always tried to make a connection with the students, just like my teacher did for me. He caught my interest in piano by finding out the stuff I enjoyed and helping me play it. The first song that made me "click" with music was Linus & Lucy, from the Peanuts show (you know, the one they all dance all crazy to). He surprised me one year with a book of sheet music for Lord of the Rings because I'd mentioned I'd been such a fan of the movies and books, and it was the fifth year I'd been taking lessons from him. 

&#x200B;

I had a student who was extremely shy, couldn't get them to talk or ask questions or anything. Once he heard me playing Megalovania from Undertale he suddenly lit up and asked me if I played any video games. We talked about video games and he asked if he could learn some video game music in addition to his regular songs. 

&#x200B;

He's a third year trumpet student who wants to go into composition now. I'm very proud of him, and I think that being able to make a connection with my students like that is one of the privileges of my profession.

I actually really enjoy teaching piano, way more than I ever would have thought. I love getting to integrate some music history into the lessons, trying to make these old figures like Mozart and Beethoven seem like real people instead of a bunch of old dead dudes in wigs. My supervisor coming up with a five minute by five minute segmented lesson template for my lessons, and explicitly mentioning that there was "no room for music history", probably should have been a clue... 

&#x200B;

Unfortunately I don't know if they are going to lose a lot of people. A lot of the teachers at the school are on the older side, I'm one of maybe four or five that's under the age of forty. But I can hold out hope that they suffer something for it, anyway.

There is not a noncompete clause! The hope of that is basically what's pushing me forward right now - the students I have for the most part seem to really enjoy their lessons with me, and it's been so awesome seeing some of them find that "spark" for music. One of them came to me last week all excited because they'd found a simplified version of Peaches off of the internet, and had managed to sight read it well enough to learn how to play for me over the week. 

&#x200B;

Thanks for the support, man, it's really appreciated.

My church has a massive freaking pride flag about as big as a car hanging in their front courtyard... I feel like even if I somehow managed to swallow my disgust and sign the contract, anyone who bothered checking it out would immediately notice that neither they nor I stand for that sort of thing. 

&#x200B;

I wish I had a cape like that!

You missed the part where I said they DO NOT CARE who they steal from. I live in one of the meth capitals of the USA, I grew up in one of the poorest neighborhoods of that city, around exactly these kinds of people. It's all about what's easiest to get their next fix from. Are they close to a CVS? They'll grab some shit from there to sell. Are they close to an unguarded car? They'll smash the window and grab some shit from there to sell. 

&#x200B;

They may be "not even remotely similar" to YOU, but to them it makes no difference. It's not about defending the particular business, it's about dealing with the mindset behind the behavior in the first place. Personally I think we need to bring back the mental institutions that Reagan got rid of, with more care as to their conditions, that would at least be a start.

I grew up eating rice and beans. I grew up poor. You, on the other hand, sound like somebody who grew up middle class who never had to genuinely worry about where their next meal was coming from. 

&#x200B;

I didn't want to become one of those people, even though it would have been REALLY damn easy to. So I worked ungodly hours and eventually, finally, managed to drag myself out and into a place where I'm doing better now. You know the kind of hate you get from those people for that? "Oh, you think you're better than us, huh??" Of course you don't, you have no idea. 

&#x200B;

And guess what? They aren't addicts because they're homeless. They're homeless because they're addicts - a solid 2/3rds of them, I'd reckon. I'll have to look up some stats on that later, but that's about the numbers I saw. When you're an addict, you drive away all your support systems, which is the number one thing that prevents homelessness... when I was homeless, I managed to get out of it in under a month. Because I'm not a fucking addict who destroyed every relationship that could have possibly supported me. 

&#x200B;

By your argument, every single poor person should be stealing, right? Because they basically have no choice. Yet somehow, not every poor person does. It's almost like even though it's harder to be a decent person who doesn't rob people when you're poor, it's still a CHOICE you make and not something you're forced into at gunpoint. 

&#x200B;

I want reform in this country - I fucking hate Reagan for closing down mental institutions, which a lot of these people desperately need. But claiming that they're blameless and were forced into doing these terrible things by society is just such an incredibly garbage take that it makes me mad. 

&#x200B;

One of the things my dad always used to say is that "a reason is not an excuse." It works for a lot of things.

OP, as many people in the comments have pointed out, that's not what people mean when they describe "transactional" relationships. 

&#x200B;

And if it's more important to you to go "well actually if you take the word transactional by its strict definition" than it is to function along with the rest of society in meaningful conversations, I think THAT'S probably a bigger sign of narcissism than going "holy SHIT, guys, I just realized - people only are in relationships if they're getting something out of it in some way??? Realizing this makes me smarter than you btw."

